{"name":"Lime Meringue","hex":"#e6ecd6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"},{"term":"meringue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture consisting of beaten egg whites and either sugar or other flavorings; it is often baked to brown the surface and may be used as a topping or in other ways, most often as a dessert.","A shell made of this mixture which serves as the receptacle for fruit, ice cream or sherbet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare as a meringue dish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meringue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meringue","blocks":[{"text":"Meringue is a type of dessert or candy, traditionally made from whipped egg whites and sugar, and occasionally an acidic ingredient such as lemon, vinegar, or cream of tartar. A binding agent such as salt, flour, or gelatin may also be added to the eggs. The key to the formation of a good meringue is the formation of stiff peaks by denaturing the protein ovalbumin via mechanical shear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Meringues_9027.jpg/330px-Meringues_9027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meringue","wikidata":"Q276276"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/500px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/1280px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg/500px-Homemade_meringues_%2816961016019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Home-made meringues","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHomemade_meringues_(16961016019).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg/500px-Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg/500px-Blancs_d%27oeufs_battus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beaten egg whites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlancs_d'oeufs_battus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png/500px-Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png/500px-Weeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beads of golden liquid can form on meringue when it is overcooked, undercooked, stored in a refrigerator, or made on a humid day.[10]  This is called weeping or sweating.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeeping_meringue_after_refrigeration.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/330px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/960px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/330px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APavlova_dessert.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/500px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/960px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg/500px-Lemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALemon_Meringue_Pie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg/500px-Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Three_meringue_mushrooms_standing_on_a_table.